          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Retirement from Politics</strong><br>\nBen-Gurion retired from political life in the late 1960s but remained active in public affairs. He moved to Kibbutz Sde Boker in the Negev Desert, symbolizing his commitment to pioneering and development. His retirement was spent writing, reflecting, and influencing the nation's path.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nDavid Ben-Gurion's legacy is a subject of respect and controversy. He is remembered as the founding father of the State of Israel, a leader who helped navigate through its tumultuous early years. His decisions and actions have left a lasting imprint on Israeli society and politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Commemoration</strong><br>\nBen-Gurion passed away in 1973. His death was mourned across Israel and by Jewish communities worldwide. He is commemorated through numerous institutions bearing his name, including Israel's main international airport and several streets, reflecting his central role in Israeli history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
